Image Reconstruction Under Corruption

You are given corrupted images and your task is to reconstruct the corresponding clean images as accurately as possible. The corruptions include a variety of degradations — masking, noise, blur, geometric distortions, and more — applied in varying combinations and strengths.

This is a prediction competition. Your system must take a corrupted image as input and produce a reconstructed image submitted as flattened pixel values in CSV format.

Evaluation

Metric: Mean Squared Error (MSE)

Your submission is scored using Mean Squared Error (MSE) computed between your reconstructed images and the hidden clean target images. Lower MSE is better (range: 0 to infinity)

    Goal: To minimize the difference between the predicted and actual images.
    Range: 0.0 to ∞ (a score of 0.0 means a perfect prediction).
    Lower is better.

